[{:db/id -1
  :readme/name "quarantine"
  :readme/org "kotoba-lang"
  :readme/role "reusable library — reversible removal (move to vault, restore exactly, purge under a retention floor)"
  :readme/note
  "A tool that removes things chosen by a heuristic should not have a delete
   opcode. quarantine moves candidates into a per-run vault with a manifest
   sufficient to restore them byte-for-byte (mode + mtime), and makes destruction
   a separate operation gated on both an explicit acknowledgement and a retention
   floor. quarantine.core is pure (manifest schema, retention arithmetic, the
   single purge gate, receipts) so the rules governing destruction are testable
   without a filesystem; quarantine.host (nbb) performs the moves.

   Moves are rename-only: a cross-device candidate is refused rather than
   downgraded to copy-then-unlink. restore! never overwrites newer state. A
   purged run keeps its manifest so the one irreversible operation is not also
   the only untraceable one. :overwrite-passes offers a best-effort overwrite
   before unlinking and the receipt records that it was attempted rather than
   certifying erasure — on APFS overwriting a path guarantees nothing about the
   old blocks.

   Extracted from gftdcojp/ai-gftd-misogi (ADR-260726, com-junkawasaki/root)."}]
